schema: "0.4"
id: fim-notebook-livebook-and-widgets-kino-js-03-teaching-exploration-widget
type: interactive
quality: high
solution: kino-js
category: notebook-livebook-and-widgets
variant: teaching-exploration-widget
prompt:
  system: |-
    You are a senior Elixir Livebook educator. Output only one complete ASCII Elixir Livebook code cell for Kino.JS and Kino.JS.Live. No Markdown fences, no prose outside code. Teach through runnable examples and concise comments.
  text: |-
    Create a teaching/exploration Livebook cell that contrasts a static Kino.JS widget with a two-way Kino.JS.Live widget. Use two small modules in one cell: one module renders deterministic data from Kino.JS.new(__MODULE__, data), and the second uses Kino.JS.Live to handle a simple event such as rating, color selection, or counter state. Include comments explaining ctx.root, ctx.pushEvent, ctx.handleEvent, JSON serialization, handle_connect, and why styles must be scoped.

    Requirements: use current module asset syntax; include Livebook compatibility and fallback behavior; include accessible labels, keyboard handling, status text, and safe use of textContent; avoid external libraries unless loaded with guarded ctx.importJS/ctx.importCSS fallback; include safe execution checks for eval, unsanitized HTML, non-serializable data, credentials, shell calls, and global CSS leaks.
  provider_options:
    max_tokens: 8192
    temperature: 0.2
    top_p: 0.9
output:
  text_format: elixir
  artifact_kind: livebook-kino-js-teaching-cell
  formats:
  - format: exs
evaluation:
  checklist:
  - "Import check: generated code installs kino and defines modules using Kino.JS and Kino.JS.Live with asset \"main.js\" blocks."
  - "Notebook compatibility: comments identify Livebook/Kino and explain that custom Kino widgets are not plain Jupyter widgets."
  - "Reactive state: the Live widget uses pushEvent, handle_event, handle_connect, and handleEvent for two-way state."
  - "Data shape: examples use small JSON-serializable data with explicit fallback defaults."
  - "Accessibility and fallback: custom controls have labels, keyboard behavior, readable status, scoped CSS, and safe text rendering."
  - "Safe execution: no obsolete constructor API, eval, unsanitized innerHTML, non-serializable data, credentials, shell calls, or style leaks."
  rubric:
  - name: Format validity
    weight: 30
    criteria:
    - "Output is one complete ASCII Elixir Livebook code cell with no Markdown fences or explanatory wrapper."
    - "The code is valid and each module uses the current Kino.JS asset pattern."
    - "Comments are concise and do not replace runnable examples."
  - name: Semantic completeness
    weight: 30
    criteria:
    - "Teaches static Kino.JS, Live two-way state, JSON data boundaries, and event flow."
    - "Uses current APIs including init(ctx, data), ctx.root, pushEvent, handleEvent, and handle_connect."
    - "Includes notebook/livebook compatibility, reactive state, data shape, accessibility, fallback, and safe execution checks."
  - name: UX/structural quality
    weight: 25
    criteria:
    - "The two examples are visually distinct, stable, and easy to compare."
    - "Labels, status text, and keyboard controls make the custom UI understandable."
    - "Scoped styles and fallback states keep the notebook output predictable."
  - name: Robustness
    weight: 15
    criteria:
    - "Handles missing data, invalid events, late clients, and optional asset failure gracefully."
    - "Avoids non-serializable terms and unsafe HTML paths."
    - "Documents when to choose higher-level Kino widgets instead of Kino.JS."
  pass_conditions:
  - "All required YAML fields are present and the generated artifact is one valid ASCII Elixir Livebook cell."
  - "Rubric weights are exactly Format validity 30, Semantic completeness 30, UX/structural quality 25, Robustness 15."
  - "The prompt requires notebook/livebook compatibility, reactive state, data shape, accessibility, fallback, and safe execution checks."
  - "The solution remains Kino.JS-centered and accurately teaches current custom-widget APIs."
tags:
- fim
- notebook-livebook-and-widgets
- livebook
- kino-js
- teaching-exploration-widget

